$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{v}(t)&=-12\sin(4t)\underset{\sim}{i}+12\cos(4t)\underset{\sim}{j}\\|\underset{\sim}{v}(t)|&=\sqrt{144\sin^2(4t)+144\cos^2(4t)}\\&=\sqrt{144}\\&=12\ \text{m s}^{-1}\end{aligned}$
The speed is independent of $t$, so it is constant.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{a}(t)&=-48\cos(4t)\underset{\sim}{i}-48\sin(4t)\underset{\sim}{j}\\&=-16\underset{\sim}{r}(t)\\\underset{\sim}{v}(t)\cdot\underset{\sim}{a}(t)&=576\sin(4t)\cos(4t)-576\cos(4t)\sin(4t)\\&=0\end{aligned}$
Both vectors are non-zero, so the zero dot product establishes perpendicularity. Acceleration points towards the centre; velocity is tangent to the circle.
At $t=0$, the diagram shows the given position and the derived directions. The arrows show directions, not vector magnitudes.
